// Lesson 2.7 — Refactor Day: Moving Stats into the StatManager
// A deliberate return to Unit 2, taught between 6.1 (jump) and 6.2 (ground
// check). The class built coins and health ON the player in 2.1/2.2 because that
// was the shortest path to a working game. It was right then and it is a problem
// now: the cave mouths in the Overworld lead to scenes that hold their own Player,
// and the next unit is where the game starts changing scenes on purpose.
//
// This is the lesson the course keeps promising: "we did not do it the best on the
// MVP, so we come back and make it better." Nothing the game DOES changes. What
// changes is which object owns the numbers.
//
// GROUND TRUTH (read from the teacher's project):
//   - StatManager.cs is a real singleton in the build: `public static
//     StatManager original;`, an Awake() with the `original != null && original
//     != this` duplicate guard, and DontDestroyOnLoad(gameObject).
//   - The three stat fields are PUBLIC (`public int health = 3; public int
//     maxHealth = 3; public int coins;`) — deliberately, because this course
//     teaches access levels with `[SerializeField]` and then lets the student
//     feel why a public field is a trade-off, not a crime.
//   - The three methods keep the names the player already had: ChangeHealth(int
//     amount), ChangeCoins(int amount), Die(). Moving a method does not rename
//     it — that is the definition of a refactor and the lesson's spine.
//   - ChangeHealth takes a SIGNED amount (the fireball calls -2, the potion
//     calls +1), caps with an if (not Mathf.Clamp), and calls Die() at <= 0.
//   - Die() resets health to maxHealth and halves coins. There is no respawn and
//     no scene reload; the player simply keeps playing. 2.2 taught a choice here;
//     this page teaches what the build does and says so.
//   - The player reaches the manager with `manager = FindAnyObjectByType<
//     StatManager>();` in Start() and a null check that logs an error. The
//     reference to the player's own copy of the fields is what disappears.
//   - StatManager lives in Start.unity and in Overworld.unity, and Start is build
//     index 0 — which is what makes the duplicate guard a real branch rather than
//     a textbook exercise.
//
// SHAPE: two level-1 walkthroughs (the manager, then the player that calls it),
// one decompose, one Socratic of three. Answer-box ids are fresh — this page did
// not exist before — so nothing positional is at risk.
