            <strong>Start (index 0)</strong>, walk into the overworld and collect one coin. The number
            in the Console is the whole story, and it is the reason for today's work.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> select the Player and read
            <strong>Inspector → Player Controller</strong>. Every number you are about to move —
            <code>Coins</code>, <code>Health</code>, <code>Max Health</code> — is a box you can watch
            change while you play. That is what you are giving up by moving them, and it is why the
            walkthrough keeps the prints.
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <table>
        <tr><th>API</th><th>What it gives you</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>public static StatManager original;</code></td>
            <td>A field that belongs to the <strong>class</strong>, not to an object — reachable with no reference and no search.</td>
            <td>It starts as <code>null</code>, and nothing claims it for you.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>void Awake()</code></td>
            <td>Unity's earliest hook: it runs once per object, and every <code>Awake()</code> in a scene finishes before the first <code>Start()</code> runs.</td>
            <td>Order between two objects' <code>Awake()</code> methods is still not promised.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>DontDestroyOnLoad(gameObject)</code></td>
            <td>The object moves into a scene of its own that never unloads, so it survives every load from then on.</td>
            <td>It takes a whole <strong>GameObject</strong>, not a component.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>FindAnyObjectByType&lt;StatManager&gt;()</code></td>
            <td>Hands back the first component of that type anywhere in the loaded scene.</td>
            <td>It searches, so it costs something — call it once, never in <code>Update()</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Debug.LogError(message)</code></td>
            <td>A Console line painted red, impossible to scroll past.</td>
            <td>It does not stop the game, so the broken behaviour still runs.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification — this is a refactor, not a rewrite.</strong>
        Nothing your game <em>does</em> changes; what changes is where the numbers live. Answer these
        before you open the files.
        <ul>
            <li><strong>The move.</strong> Which fields leave <code>PlayerController</code>, and which stay — and why does the one you keep belong to the player rather than to the run?</li>
            <li><strong>The owner.</strong> Which setup hook claims the slot, and what does its other branch do with a second copy?</li>
            <li><strong>The seam.</strong> Which single method may change health, and what ceiling does it enforce?</li>
            <li><strong>The callers.</strong> How will you find every place that used to reach for those fields — by reading the file, or by deleting them first?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Who Owns a Number?</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Your stat fields are <code>public</code> after this refactor, where September taught you <code>[SerializeField] private</code>. What does <code>public</code> let another script do that a method call would not — and why might that be a problem in a project with more than one programmer?</li>
            <li><code>Start (index 0)</code> already contains its own StatManager object. If you walk back to it later, what will be there — and what happens if nothing stops it from registering itself a second time?</li>
            <li>When you delete a field and let the compiler list your call sites, you are using a tool to find something you could have searched for by hand. Which is more reliable, and what does the answer tell you about why refactoring is usually a two-step job — delete, then compile?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

